    if (label) label.textContent = t.title;
  } else {
    // Create new node
    li = document.createElement('li');
    ...
    todoList.insertBefore(li, insertBefore);
  }
  insertBefore = li;
}
```

OH WAIT. There's a bug! The first loop removes ALL existing items from the DOM. Then the second loop tries to find them in `existingItems` and update them in place — but they've already been removed from the DOM! The line:

```js
existingItems[id].parentNode.removeChild(existingItems[id]);
```

removes EVERY exiting li from the DOM. Then later, for items that still exist in the filtered list, the code tries to re-use those existing items. But they've already been removed from the DOM, and they're NOT re-appended to the todoList (only newly created items get `todoList.insertBefore(li, insertBefore)`).

So for the items that ARE in both the DOM and the filtered list, the code updates their class/toggle but doesn't re-append them. Since they've been removed from the DOM, they're now detached.

The fix is: the stale-node removal loop should only remove nodes for items that are NOT in the filtered list, OR the updated items should be re-appended.

